# Spring Boot — Dependency Injection & IoC 🌱

When I first started Spring Boot, I kept seeing things like:

```java
@Service
@Autowired
```

and constructor parameters that Spring somehow "provided".

The important question was:

> **Who creates these objects, and how does Spring know what to put where?**

This is where **IoC (Inversion of Control)** and **Dependency Injection (DI)** come in.

---

# 1. What is a Dependency?

Suppose I have:

```java
class StudentController {

    StudentService service;
}
```

The `StudentController` needs `StudentService` to do its work.

So:

```text
StudentController
       ↓
needs
       ↓
StudentService
```

`StudentService` is a **dependency** of `StudentController`.

---

# 2. Without Dependency Injection

Normally in Java, I could create the object myself:

```java
class StudentController {

    StudentService service;

    StudentController() {
        service = new StudentService();
    }
}
```

Here, the controller is responsible for creating its dependency.

```text
StudentController
       │
       └── creates → StudentService
```

This works, but it creates tight coupling.

---

# 3. What Spring does differently

With Spring, instead of the controller creating the service, Spring can create and provide it.

```java
@Service
public class StudentService {
}
```

Then:

```java
@RestController
public class StudentController {

    private final StudentService service;

    public StudentController(StudentService service) {
        this.service = service;
    }
}
```

Now the controller doesn't say:

```java
new StudentService()
```

Spring provides the object.

```text
             Spring
               │
               ↓
        StudentService
               │
               ↓
       StudentController
```

This is **Dependency Injection**.

---

# 4. What is IoC?

IoC means:

> **Inversion of Control**

Normally, my code controls object creation:

```text
My Code
   ↓
new StudentService()
```

With Spring:

```text
Spring Container
       ↓
creates/manages StudentService
       ↓
provides it to my code
```

So the control over object creation and wiring is moved to the framework.

That's the basic idea behind **Inversion of Control**.

---

# 5. Dependency Injection vs IoC

These two terms are related, but they aren't exactly the same thing.

### IoC

A broader idea:

> The framework takes control of certain responsibilities that the application would otherwise manage itself.

### Dependency Injection

One way of implementing that idea:

> Dependencies are provided to an object instead of the object creating them itself.

So:

```text
IoC
 ↓
Dependency Injection
 ↓
Spring provides required objects
```

---

# 6. What is a Spring Bean?

A **Bean** is an object that is created and managed by the Spring IoC container.

For example:

```java
@Service
public class StudentService {
}
```

Spring can create a `StudentService` object and manage it as a Bean.

Conceptually:

```text
Spring Container
       │
       ├── StudentService Bean
       ├── StudentController Bean
       └── Other Beans
```

The important part is:

> Not every Java object is automatically a Spring Bean.

It needs to be registered with the Spring container, commonly through annotations such as:

```java
@Component
@Service
@Repository
@Controller
@RestController
```

or through explicit `@Bean` methods.

---

# 7. `@Component`

`@Component` is a general-purpose stereotype annotation.

Example:

```java
@Component
public class EmailService {
}
```

Spring can detect this class during component scanning and create a Bean from it.

---

# 8. `@Service`

`@Service` is commonly used for the service/business logic layer.

```java
@Service
public class StudentService {

    public String getStudent() {
        return "Student";
    }
}
```

Technically, `@Service` is a specialized form of `@Component`.

So Spring can discover it as a Bean.

---

# 9. `@Repository`

`@Repository` is commonly used for persistence/database-related classes.

```java
@Repository
public class StudentRepository {
}
```

It communicates the role of the class clearly.

With Spring Data JPA, you may often see:

```java
public interface StudentRepository
        extends JpaRepository<Student, Long> {
}
```

---

# 10. `@Controller` and `@RestController`

For web applications:

```java
@Controller
```

is commonly used for MVC controllers.

For REST APIs:

```java
@RestController
```

is commonly used.

A simplified way to remember:

```text
@Component
    ↓
General Spring Bean

@Service
    ↓
Business logic

@Repository
    ↓
Persistence

@Controller
    ↓
Web controller

@RestController
    ↓
REST API controller
```

---

# 11. Constructor Injection

This is the style I want to get comfortable with.

```java
@Service
public class StudentService {
}
```

Then:

```java
@RestController
public class StudentController {

    private final StudentService studentService;

    public StudentController(StudentService studentService) {
        this.studentService = studentService;
    }
}
```

Spring sees that `StudentController` requires a `StudentService`.

If there is a suitable Spring Bean, Spring supplies it through the constructor.

---

# 12. Why `final`?

Notice:

```java
private final StudentService studentService;
```

The reference is assigned through the constructor:

```java
public StudentController(StudentService studentService) {
    this.studentService = studentService;
}
```

After construction, the reference can't be reassigned.

This makes the dependency explicit and helps communicate:

> This controller requires a StudentService.

---

# 13. What about `@Autowired`?

You may also see:

```java
@Autowired
public StudentController(StudentService studentService) {
    this.studentService = studentService;
}
```

In modern Spring, if the class has a **single constructor**, `@Autowired` is generally not necessary.

So this:

```java
public StudentController(StudentService studentService) {
    this.studentService = studentService;
}
```

is enough.

This is one reason constructor injection is clean.

---

# 14. Field Injection

Another style is:

```java
@Autowired
private StudentService studentService;
```

This is called **field injection**.

It is short, but constructor injection is generally preferred for required dependencies because the dependency is explicit and the object can be constructed with all required dependencies.

Compare:

```java
@Autowired
private StudentService service;
```

with:

```java
private final StudentService service;

public StudentController(StudentService service) {
    this.service = service;
}
```

I prefer the second style for understanding what's actually happening.

---

# 15. Constructor Injection vs Field Injection

| Constructor Injection                          | Field Injection                            |
| ---------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------ |
| Dependency is visible in constructor           | Dependency is hidden in a field            |
| Can use `final`                                | Usually cannot use `final` in the same way |
| Object requires dependency during construction | Dependency is injected after construction  |
| Easier to reason about                         | Shorter                                    |
| Commonly preferred                             | Often seen in older/tutorial code          |

---

# 16. Multiple Dependencies

A controller can have more than one dependency.

```java
@RestController
public class StudentController {

    private final StudentService studentService;
    private final EmailService emailService;

    public StudentController(
            StudentService studentService,
            EmailService emailService) {

        this.studentService = studentService;
        this.emailService = emailService;
    }
}
```

Now Spring has to provide both:

```text
Spring Container
      │
      ├── StudentService
      │
      └── EmailService
             │
             ↓
      StudentController
```

---

# 17. What if Spring finds multiple Beans?

This is where things get interesting.

Suppose:

```java
@Component
class GmailService implements EmailService {
}
```

and:

```java
@Component
class OutlookService implements EmailService {
}
```

Now:

```java
public StudentController(EmailService emailService) {
    this.emailService = emailService;
}
```

Spring sees:

```text
EmailService
    │
    ├── GmailService
    └── OutlookService
```

There are multiple candidates.

Spring needs to know which one should be injected.

This is where `@Primary` or `@Qualifier` can be used.

---

# 18. `@Primary`

Example:

```java
@Primary
@Component
public class GmailService implements EmailService {
}
```

Now if Spring needs an `EmailService`, it can prefer the `GmailService` Bean.

Conceptually:

```text
EmailService
     │
     ├── GmailService ← Primary
     │
     └── OutlookService
```

---

# 19. `@Qualifier`

Another option is to explicitly specify which Bean should be used.

```java
@Component("gmail")
public class GmailService implements EmailService {
}
```

```java
@Component("outlook")
public class OutlookService implements EmailService {
}
```

Then:

```java
public StudentController(
        @Qualifier("gmail") EmailService emailService) {

    this.emailService = emailService;
}
```

Now there is no ambiguity.

---

# 20. Where does the Spring Container come in?

The **Spring IoC container** is responsible for managing Beans and their dependencies.

A simplified picture:

```text
              Spring IoC Container
                       │
        ┌──────────────┼──────────────┐
        ↓              ↓              ↓
   Controller       Service       Repository
        │              │              │
        └──────────────┴──────────────┘
                  Dependencies
```

In Spring, the container is commonly represented by an `ApplicationContext`.

---

# 21. ApplicationContext

You may encounter:

```java
ApplicationContext context =
        SpringApplication.run(Application.class, args);
```

The `ApplicationContext` represents the Spring application context and provides access to the Beans managed by Spring.

For example, conceptually:

```java
StudentService service =
        context.getBean(StudentService.class);
```

This asks the container for a `StudentService` Bean.

Normally, in application code, I would rely on dependency injection rather than repeatedly calling `getBean()` manually.

---

# 22. The complete flow

Suppose we have:

```java
@Service
public class StudentService {
}
```

and:

```java
@RestController
public class StudentController {

    private final StudentService studentService;

    public StudentController(StudentService studentService) {
        this.studentService = studentService;
    }
}
```

When the application starts, conceptually:

```text
Spring Boot starts
       ↓
ApplicationContext created
       ↓
Component scanning
       ↓
StudentService detected
       ↓
StudentService Bean created
       ↓
StudentController detected
       ↓
Spring sees StudentController needs StudentService
       ↓
Spring injects StudentService
       ↓
Controller is ready
```

This is the part that helped me understand what all those annotations are actually doing.

---

# 23. Why DI is useful

Without DI:

```text
Controller
    ↓
creates Service
    ↓
Service creates Repository
    ↓
Repository creates database-related objects
```

Classes become responsible for creating too many things.

With DI:

```text
             Spring
               │
       manages dependencies
               │
       ┌───────┼────────┐
       ↓       ↓        ↓
 Controller  Service  Repository
```

The classes mainly focus on their own responsibilities.

---

# 24. Dependency Injection also helps testing

Suppose:

```java
public StudentController(StudentService studentService) {
    this.studentService = studentService;
}
```

For testing, I can provide a different implementation or a mock of `StudentService`.

Conceptually:

```text
Production:
Controller → Real StudentService

Test:
Controller → Mock StudentService
```

The controller doesn't have to know how the service was created.

This is one of the practical benefits of dependency injection.

---

# 25. DI is not the same as `new`

This is a useful distinction.

Normal Java:

```java
StudentService service = new StudentService();
```

Here **my code** controls object creation.

Spring DI:

```java
public StudentController(StudentService service) {
    this.service = service;
}
```

Here the object is provided by the Spring container.

So:

```text
new
↓
I create the dependency


Dependency Injection
↓
Dependency is provided to me
```

---

# 26. My mental model

When I see:

```java
@Service
class StudentService {
}
```

I think:

```text
"Spring, manage an instance of this class."
```

When I see:

```java
private final StudentService service;

public StudentController(StudentService service) {
    this.service = service;
}
```

I think:

```text
"StudentController needs StudentService.
Spring, provide it."
```

And when Spring starts:

```text
Application
    ↓
Spring Container
    ↓
creates/manages Beans
    ↓
connects dependencies
    ↓
Application is ready
```

---

# Quick Revision

```text
IoC
→ Control over object creation/management is given to the framework.

Dependency
→ An object/class that another class needs.

Dependency Injection
→ Providing that dependency instead of creating it inside the class.

Bean
→ Object managed by the Spring container.

@Component
→ General Spring-managed component.

@Service
→ Usually used for business/service logic.

@Repository
→ Usually used for persistence/database logic.

@RestController
→ REST web controller.

Constructor Injection
→ Dependency is provided through the constructor.

@Autowired
→ Tells Spring where dependency injection is needed;
  with a single constructor, it is generally not required.

@Primary
→ Gives preference to one Bean when multiple candidates exist.

@Qualifier
→ Explicitly selects which Bean should be injected.

ApplicationContext
→ Spring's application context/container interface used to
  manage and access Beans.
```

# The main thing I want to remember

> **IoC is the idea. Dependency Injection is one way Spring implements that idea.**

And instead of:

```java
new StudentService();
```

I should start thinking:

```text
"StudentController needs StudentService.
Spring can provide it."
```

That is the basic idea behind **Dependency Injection in Spring Boot**.
